  "narrative": "The central dataset-specific map is now sufficiently established that the next explore step should not repeatedly retest the main SMARCA4, KEAP1, TP53, and PTEN effects as originally formulated. SMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with stronger dependency, KEAP1 and TP53 losses are associated with weaker dependency, and PTEN loss is a secondary context-dependent association with stronger dependency, especially outside TP53-loss contexts. The remaining scientific questions are whether the central effects are robust to additional molecular backgrounds, whether secondary predictors reveal biologically meaningful context-specific signals, and whether measured expression, chromosomal, or assay-score features modify dependency. For direction interpretation: under direction -1, a positive signed estimate means the raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is negative, i.e. the exposed group is more negative on dependency_composite and therefore has stronger dependency; under direction +1, a positive signed estimate means the raw contrast is positive, i.e. less negative dependency and weaker dependency. For interaction claims, the raw contrast is the subgroup exposed-minus-comparator difference minus the complement exposed-minus-comparator difference. A signed positive estimate under direction -1 means the exposure effect is more negative, or stronger in the dependency direction, in the subgroup than in the complement; a signed positive estimate under direction +1 means the exposure effect is more positive, or stronger in the weaker-dependency direction, in the subgroup.  I first propose SMARCA4 loss among cells that are TP53-intact and KEAP1-intact. This is a more co-alteration-restricted extension of the accepted SMARCA4-loss association and asks whether the strong SMARCA4 signal persists in a background lacking the two strongest opposing co-alterations. Expected raw effect is negative, direction -1, because SMARCA4 loss has consistently been associated with stronger dependency. Plausible alternatives are attenuation in this background or complete additive independence.  Second and third, I propose effect modification tests for KEAP1 loss by hypoxia and TP53 loss by stemness, each within SMARCA4-intact cells. KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker dependency overall; if hypoxic cells have higher oxidative-stress pathway activity, KEAP1 loss may show a stronger positive raw contrast, direction +1. Similarly, if high stemness reflects a distinct cell-state context, TP53 loss may show a stronger positive raw contrast, direction +1. These are not retests of broad main effects; they test whether the accepted weaker-dependency associations are state-modified. Null interactions are plausible, and opposite-direction modification is possible if those states reduce dependency heterogeneity.  Fourth and fifth, I propose PTEN-loss interaction contexts: PTEN loss by high MYC expression and by high research_signature_a, each among TP53-intact cells. PTEN loss has accepted associations with stronger dependency in TP53-intact backgrounds, with independent validation, but the broad association remains unresolved and secondary. High MYC expression is a plausible biological modifier because PI3K/PTEN and MYC pathways interact in growth regulation; I expect PTEN loss to show a more negative raw contrast, hence stronger dependency, in high-MYC cells than in low-MYC cells, direction -1. The research_signature_a test is a more agnostic completeness probe of whether an assay score modifies the same TP53-intact PTEN association. Again, null is highly plausible because PTEN is a secondary post-hoc signal.  Sixth through eleventh, I propose secondary-predictor context tests: APC loss by WNT activity, CDKN2A loss by aneuploidy, MYC amplification by MYC expression, SMARCA4 loss by EMT within TP53/KEAP1-intact cells, BRCA2 loss by IFN-gamma activity, and NF1 loss by EMT. APC loss is not yet evaluated broadly, but it is biologically tied to WNT signaling; I hypothesize APC loss is associated with stronger dependency preferentially in high WNT activity, direction -1. CDKN2A loss is borderline below the 0.15-unit threshold broadly; I hypothesize a stronger negative raw effect in higher-aneuploidy cells, direction -1. MYC amplification was not supported broadly; I hypothesize that amplification may matter specifically when MYC expression is high, direction -1. SMARCA4 loss is already very robust, but I test EMT effect modification in a TP53/KEAP1-intact background to ask whether mesenchymal state amplifies the dependency association. BRCA2 loss and NF1 loss are low-prevalence untested predictors; I test plausible high-immune or mesenchymal states, respectively. For all interaction proposals, I use median-based high groups to preserve adequate cell sizes and to avoid unstable upper-quartile rare-marker intersections. These are dataset-specific association and effect-modification hypotheses, not causal claims or claims of generalizability.",
