tronger dependency. Plausible alternatives are attenuation in this background or complete additive independence.  Second and third, I propose effect modification tests for KEAP1 loss by hypoxia and TP53 loss by stemness, each within SMARCA4-intact cells. KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker dependency overall; if hypoxic cells have higher oxidative-stress pathway activity, KEAP1 loss may show a stronger positive raw contrast, direction +1. Similarly, if high stemness reflects a distinct cell-state context, TP53 loss may show a stronger positive raw contrast, direction +1. These are not retests of broad main effects; they test whether the accepted weaker-dependency associations are state-modified. Null interactions are plausible, and opposite-direction modification is possible if those states reduce dependency heterogeneity.  Fourth and fifth, I propose PTEN-loss interaction contexts: PTEN loss by high MYC expression and by high research_signature_a, each among TP53-intact cells. PTEN loss has accepted associations with stronger dependency in TP53-intact backgrounds, with independent validation, but the broad association remains unresolved and secondary. High MYC expression is a plausible biological modifier because PI3K/PTEN and MYC pathways interact in growth regulation; I expect PTEN loss to show a more negative raw contrast, hence stronger dependency, in high-MYC cells than in low-MYC cells, direction -1. The research_signature_a test is a more agnostic completeness probe of whether an assay score modifies the same TP53-intact PTEN association. Again, null is highly plausible because PTEN is a secondary post-hoc signal.  Sixth through eleventh, I propose secondary-predictor context tests: APC loss by WNT activity, CDKN2A loss by aneuploidy, MYC amplification by MYC expression, SMARCA4 loss by EMT within TP53/KEAP1-intact cells, BRCA2 loss by IFN-gamma activity, and NF1 loss by EMT. APC loss is not yet evaluated broadly, but it is biologically tied to WNT signaling; I hypothesize APC loss is associated with stronger dependency preferentially in high WNT activity, direction -1. CDKN2A loss is borderline below the 0.15-unit threshold broadly; I hypothesize a stronger negative raw effect in higher-aneuploidy cells, direction -1. MYC amplification was not supported broadly; I hypothesize that amplification may matter specifically when MYC expression is high, direction -1. SMARCA4 loss is already very robust, but I test EMT effect modification in a TP53/KEAP1-intact background to ask whether mesenchymal state amplifies the dependency association. BRCA2 loss and NF1 loss are low-prevalence untested predictors; I test plausible high-immune or mesenchymal states, respectively. For all interaction proposals, I use median-based high groups to preserve adequate cell sizes and to avoid unstable upper-quartile rare-marker intersections. These are dataset-specific association and effect-modification hypotheses, not causal claims or claims of generalizability.",
