  "narrative": "The dominant dataset-specific predictor map is already sufficiently supported: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger dependency, while KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss are associated with weaker dependency. PTEN loss appears secondary and context-dependent, with stronger dependency most consistently among TP53-intact or low-mask backgrounds. I therefore avoid further broad retests of SMARCA4, KEAP1, TP53, and PTEN main effects and register comparisons that test two remaining scientific questions: completeness of the predictor landscape and whether accepted or borderline associations are modified by measured cell-state or assay features. The first batch tests unexamined technical or experimental covariates: RNA-omics availability, HUMANGNE_CD library status, and high versus lower serum concentration. These are not primary biological hypotheses but are important because missingness, library construction, or culture conditions could create dataset-specific shifts in composite dependency scores. A positive raw contrast under the registered +1 direction would mean the exposed category has a less negative dependency score, i.e., weaker dependency; a negative raw contrast would mean stronger dependency.  The second batch tests whether the central dependency associations are state-modified in scientifically plausible backgrounds. SMARCA4 loss is expected to remain associated with much stronger dependency, but I test whether that association is amplified in high-hypoxia cells relative to low-hypoxia cells while restricting the eligible population to TP53-intact and KEAP1-intact cells, thereby excluding the two strongest opposing co-alterations. KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss are both associated with weaker dependency; I test whether their positive raw contrast is stronger in high-stemness or high-EMT cells respectively, while restricting away from the strongest opposing marker contexts. These are not retests of broad main effects; they test whether cell-state features change the magnitude of the association. Null additive behavior is a very plausible result, so interaction claims require both a meaningful point estimate and an interval that excludes scientifically negligible values.  The third batch tests PTEN as a secondary modifier-sensitive predictor. PTEN loss is already associated with stronger dependency among TP53-intact cells, but the broad PTEN association remains unresolved. I test PTEN-loss interactions by high MYC expression and high WNT activity within TP53-intact and KEAP1-intact cells because PI3K/PTEN biology may be modified by growth-signaling or transcriptional state. The direction is negative for the raw interaction, which under direction -1 yields a positive signed estimate if PTEN loss is associated with a more negative dependency contrast in the high-MYC or high-WNT subgroup than in the low-MYC or low-WNT complement. This would support effect modification; a null result would support additive independence.  The fourth batch probes secondary molecular predictors using measured pathway or genomic-state modifiers. APC mutation is tested by high versus low WNT activity because APC loss is mechanistically linked to WNT signaling, even though APC mutation has not yet produced a broad dependency association. CDKN2A loss was borderline below the 0.15-unit threshold in marginal and restricted analyses, so I test whether high aneuploidy amplifies its association. MYC amplification was null broadly, so I test whether it becomes relevant in cells with high MYC expression. STK11 loss was null broadly, so I test whether high hypoxia reveals a context-specific dependency association. All continuous modifiers are dichotomized at approximate medians to preserve adequate cell sizes and avoid unstable rare-marker intersections. These are dataset-specific association and effect-modification hypotheses only; they do not claim causality, pathway activation, or generalizability beyond the supplied cells."
