y association persists in adenocarcinoma. The signed estimate is +0.742, interval +0.605 to +0.878, far above threshold. I accept and close H109. This is consistent with the already validated broad MSI-high effect and supports that the signal is not driven only by the minority non-adenocarcinoma cells, but it remains a dataset-specific association.  Growth-rate interactions did not support the registered hypotheses. H113, SMARCA4-loss amplification in faster-growing cells, has signed estimate -0.136, interval -0.286 to +0.015; it is not in the claimed positive direction and is not threshold-sized. H114, TP53-loss attenuation in faster-growing cells, has signed estimate -0.107, interval -0.320 to +0.107. H115, KEAP1-loss attenuation in faster-growing cells, has signed estimate +0.097, interval -0.046 to +0.241. None provide robust evidence of a threshold-sized modification, so I reject and close all three. These rejections indicate lack of supported evidence in this dataset, not proof that the true interaction is exactly zero.  The remaining PTEN transcriptomic interactions were also unsupported. H74, PTEN loss by MYC expression state, is near null: -0.003, interval -0.182 to +0.177. H75, PTEN loss by high Wnt activity, has estimate +0.145, interval -0.035 to +0.324; the point is near threshold but the interval includes zero and the point is not robustly threshold-sized, so I reject and close. H79, STK11 loss by hypoxia state, has estimate +0.060, interval -0.094 to +0.215, and is unsupported. The batch diagnostic H116 is also unsupported: batch_a versus batch_b has signed estimate -0.011, interval -0.096 to +0.073. This does not rule out all batch-related variation, but it does not support a threshold-sized difference for this particular batch contrast.  The most informative next independent validation target is broad PTEN loss, H32. Several PTEN contexts are already accepted, but the whole-population PTEN claim remains unresolved because its discovery lower bound, 