  "narrative": "Validation for H111 was assessed at the response deadline. H111 tests PTEN loss in STK11-intact, TP53-intact cells in the stronger-dependency direction, and the reported estimates are already signed to that hypothesis. Discovery gave +0.2351 with 95% interval +0.1363 to +0.3339 (R109), and independent validation gave +0.2630 with adjusted interval +0.1053 to +0.4208 (R130). The point estimates are directionally concordant and exceed the 0.15 composite-unit threshold, but both lower bounds remain below threshold, and the validation lower bound is well below threshold. Therefore H111 remains unresolved and I close its investigation: the evidence supports a suggestive moderate PTEN-loss association in this background, but not a robust threshold-sized association. This closes the last due PTEN follow-up and reinforces that broad PTEN loss should not be treated as a robust main predictor. The stable supported dataset-specific map is: SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger dependency, with discovery +0.8325 (R5) and validation +0.7976 (R13); TP53 loss is associated with weaker dependency, with discovery +0.4232 (R2) and validation +0.3985 (R39); KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker dependency, with discovery +0.8315 (R4) and validation +0.7648 (R92); and MSI-high is associated with stronger dependency, with discovery +0.7918 (R56) and validation +0.7690 (R78). These core associations remain accepted across multiple contexts, including after excluding MSI-high and within adherent growth, adenocarcinoma, and several genetic backgrounds; within-stratum acceptance shows presence but not effect modification. PTEN loss is a secondary association accepted only in selected backgrounds, including TP53-intact cells (R34, R53), KEAP1-intact cells (R35, R105), TP53/KEAP1-intact cells (R49), TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-intact cells (R29), TP53/SMARCA4-intact cells (R86), KEAP1-loss TP53-intact cells (R51, R96), adenocarcinoma TP53/SMARCA4-intact cells (R97), non-MSI TP53/KEAP1-intact cells (R142), and non-MSI triple-intact cells (R151). These accepted PTEN contexts are overlapping dataset-specific associations, not independent causal mechanisms. Negative or unsupported comparisons include KRAS mutation, overall STK11 loss, MYC amplification, PIK3CA mutation, ARID1A loss, MTAP main effects, RB1 loss, SMAD4 loss, NF1 loss, BRCA2 loss, BRAF V600E, EGFR amplification, ERBB2 amplification, APC mutation, research markers D/E/F, omics-availability and CRISPR-library indicators, media serum, and batch_a versus batch_b. Tested interactions with lineage subtype, growth rate, mutation burden, screen quality, Wnt activity, aneuploidy, MYC expression, Cas9 activity, growth pattern, and related contexts were generally unsupported, while some MSI or 8q24 interaction analyses were invalid because of empty cells. Remaining uncertainty is concentrated in moderate PTEN contexts, especially STK11-loss backgrounds, and exploratory co-deletion or context interactions. The most informative already registered next discovery is H146, MTAP loss among CDKN2A-loss cells, because MTAP main effects were unsupported but 9p21 co-deletion context remains plausible. Further PTEN follow-up should prioritize better-powered STK11-loss analyses or new independent validation; no validation budget remains, so additional broad PTEN claims should not be promoted. All conclusions are dataset-specific associations on a composite dependency endpoint, not causal or externally generalizable claims.",
