e opposite stronger-dependency claim H2 was rejected. KEAP1 loss is robustly associated with weaker composite dependency: signed overall discovery estimate +0.8315, lower bound +0.7658 to +0.8972 (R4), and independent validation estimate +0.7648, lower bound +0.6687 to +0.8609 (R92); the opposite stronger-dependency claim H4 was rejected. MSI-high status is robustly associated with stronger composite dependency: signed overall discovery estimate +0.7918, lower bound +0.6763 to +0.9072 (R56), and independent validation estimate +0.7690, lower bound +0.5936 to +0.9443 (R78). These conclusions are associations with measured predictor status within the supplied composite-dependency dataset, not causal single-gene knockout effects, and not external biological generalization claims.\n\nThe core map is also context-presistent across many measured strata. In the newly assessed 2% serum context, TP53 loss remained associated with weaker dependency (+0.424, lower +0.190; R198), SMARCA4 loss with stronger dependency (+0.848, lower +0.680; R199), KEAP1 loss with weaker dependency (+0.910, lower +0.754; R200), and MSI-high with stronger dependency (+0.743, lower +0.503; R201). Within MYC-amplification cells, the same core predictors remained threshold-robust: SMARCA4 loss stronger (+0.791, lower +0.636; R203), TP53 loss weaker (+0.676, lower +0.443; R204), and KEAP1 loss weaker (+0.901, lower +0.725; R205). These are context presence tests, not formal effect-modification claims.\n\nPTEN loss remains the main secondary signal. Several measured PTEN-loss contexts are accepted, including TP53-intact cells (R34 with validation R53), KEAP1-intact cells (R35 with validation R105), KEAP1-loss cells (R51), TP53/KEAP1-intact cells (R49), MSI-negative TP53/KEAP1-intact cells (R142), and TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-intact cells (R151). However, the overall PTEN-loss claim and several key borderline contexts, including H32, H111, and H124, do not have lower bounds above the 0.15 threshold. Because PTEN has been examined across many correlated co-mutation and molecular contexts and the voluntary validation budget is exhausted, I retain PTEN loss as a secondary association with unresolved context dependence rather than promoting it to a broad threshold-robust overall claim.\n\nNegative dataset-specific findings include no scientifically meaningful dependency_composite differences for KRAS mutation (R1), STK11 main effect (R3), MYC amplification main effect (R6), PIK3CA mutation (R38), ARID1A main effect (R17), MTAP main effect and MTAP in CDKN2A-loss cells (R18, R79, R154), RB1 loss (R21), SMAD4 loss (R22), NF1 loss (R57), BRCA2 loss (R55), BRAF V600E (R59), EGFR amplification (R60, R106), ERBB2 amplification (R61), APC mutation and APC interaction by WNT activity (R58, R128), research markers D/E/F positivity (R7, R8, R54), omics-availability indicators (R28, R77, R76), CRISPR-library main effects (R9, R62, R75), media-serum main-effect contrasts (R63, R196, R197), and screen batch (R115). CDKN2A loss is not a broadly accepted stronger-dependency effect; its favorable main effect R19 had lower bound +0.062, and most subgroup or interaction contexts were unresolved or rejected. MYC amplification among high-MYC-expression cells was near null and rejected (R202), although the direct formal MYC-by-MYC-expression interaction H78 remains a separate unresolved interaction question. Most formal interaction tests, including co-mutation, lineage, growth, screen-QC, Cas9, expression, ploidy, aneuploidy, and mutation-burden modifiers, were rejected or invalid. The dataset therefore supports robust presence of core associations across many strata more than it supports reliable effect modification.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty is concentrated in low-power or repeatedly searched contexts: PTEN-loss in STK11/TP53-intact cells (H111), MSI-negative cells (H124), and overall (H32); TP53-loss in low-serum media (H148), KY-library cells (H151), ARID1A-loss cells (H166), and MSI-high cells (H172); MYC-amplification interaction with expression and copy-number-defined MYC contexts; KEAP1 interaction by adherent growth; and some CDKN2A secondary effects. Closing these investigations is a resource-allocation and multiplicity-control decision, not evidence that their point estimates are null.\n\nNo new validation can be requested because the voluntary validation budget is exhausted, and no new proposals are registered now because further subgroup mining would mainly add multiplicity risk without independent confirmation. If confirmatory capacity were available, the most informative next analyses would be targeted independent validation of the borderline PTEN contexts H111 and H124, low-power TP53-loss contexts H148, H151, H166, and H172, and a pre-specified joint assessment of the core SMARCA4, TP53, KEAP1, and MSI effects with strict control of multiplicity. For now, the defensible conclusion is a dataset-specific association map: SMARCA4 loss with stronger composite dependency, TP53 loss with weaker composite dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker composite dependency, and MSI-high with stronger composite dependency, while PTEN loss remains secondary and context-dependent.",
